2026-11-28T06:48:19.7830685Z    image gate: house_2123_prairie chicagology_armour_photo: verdict "differs", 2 difference(s) open: the mansard over the bow (re-read for T-3725 by silhouette, column by column, print against render): there is no step in the deck. The print's roof top (its dark mass, cresting aside) stands level at y 313-322 px from x 1700 to 1850 and drops to y 340 at x 1860, where the render's flat-faced curb runs down from y 311 to 339 over the same columns; the two agree from x 1860 to 1910 (y 340-348 against 341-351). That level line is what a curb carried out round the bow draws: through this camera a curb at the main curb's height (15.15 m) following the bow's arc (chord 3.9 m, rise 0.93 m, set in from the fascia as the main face's is) projects at y 307-316 from x 1703 to 1849 and drops to y 344 at x 1872, within about 8 px of the print's everywhere. So the mansard's street face bows out over the bow, its curb on the bow's line, and the print's taller cresting (posts at about y 287-300) stands on that bowed curb and stops on a post at about x 1857, the bow's south end; south of it the curb runs bare to the second street dormer. Building it needs K01 to carry the mansard's street face round a K08 bow (its rings following the arc, the street dormer at 3.6 m set on the bowed face and turned with it, the cresting along the arc), which K01 cannot do yet. The earlier reading (a deck stepping 0.45 m down south of the bow, or a front crown over the bow's stretch) is withdrawn: a front crown raises the curb, and the print's curb does not rise; the front's south end at the print's right edge (x 1960-2000 px, y 410-530): the mansard's face turns down there, about where the 1911 sheet cuts the front's south-west corner back 1.9 m round the entrance (v 7.2 m); the render carries the full-width mansard and front past the frame (liberty L-k01-2123-prairie-3724, the cut corner not built). By silhouette the print's roof is gone from x 1960 px: sky down to y 393-399 at x 1960-1980, where the render's curb stands at y 351-364, so the mansard stops at about 6.9 m along the front, about where the sheet's cut begins (7.2 m); the corniced, crested head the earlier reading gave the second street dormer (x 1910-1990, cornice y 390-410) is likely that cut corner's own cornice and cresting turning back
